  "turn": {
    "pericope": "Matthew 13:24-30, 36-43",
    "refKey": "Matt.13.24-Matt.13.43",
    "gravity": "Wisdom says God judges with mildness and great forbearance, and so teaches his people that the righteous must be kind; Paul hears the whole creation groaning in hope; and Jesus tells of a field where wheat and weeds grow tangled until harvest. The nerve: how to live faithfully in a world — and a church — that will not sort cleanly into good and evil.",
    "trap": "The parable gets read as a shrug (\"don't judge anything\") or, flipped, as a mandate for the zealous to start pulling weeds — purging the church, the family, the world of the wrong people. Both miss that the restraint is the King's command and the sorting is the King's alone.",
    "hinge": "The weeds are real — the enemy's own work, not to be pretended away — and you are forbidden to pull them, because your hands cannot tell wheat from weed without tearing up both. Real evil, real judgment, and your task in the meantime is patience, not harvest.",
    "doors": [
      {
        "landing": "Let both grow",
        "claim": "You are not the reaper — pulling the weeds now would tear up wheat with them, so the command is a hard patience that leaves room for the weed to become wheat.",
        "witnesses": [
          {
            "author": "Jerome",
            "tradition": "latin-patristic",
            "work": "Catena Aurea",
            "mode": "text",
            "reading": "Room for repentance is left, and we are warned not to cut off a brother hastily, since one who today is corrupted by error may tomorrow be set right."
          },
          {
            "author": "John Chrysostom",
            "tradition": "greek-patristic",
            "work": "Catena Aurea",
            "mode": "text",
            "reading": "This the Lord spoke to forbid any putting to death; for we ought not to kill even a heretic, since so a never-ending war would be brought into the world."
          }
        ],
        "say": "Your hands are not steady enough to weed God's field; the patience that galls you is mercy leaving a door open.",
        "do": "Name one person they have already written off as a weed, and ask them to keep the door of repentance open instead of uprooting."
      },
      {
        "landing": "While they slept",
        "claim": "The weeds came in the unguarded hour, sown by an enemy and not by God — which both names the origin of evil and calls the sleepers to wake.",
        "witnesses": [
          {
            "author": "Augustine",
            "tradition": "latin-patristic",
            "work": "Catena Aurea",
            "mode": "text",
            "reading": "\"While men slept\" — while the leaders of the Church lay in supineness, and after the Apostles had fallen into the sleep of death, then the enemy came."
          },
          {
            "author": "John Wesley",
            "tradition": "wesleyan",
            "work": "Explanatory Notes",
            "mode": "text",
            "reading": "While men slept — they ought to have watched; the Lord of the field never sleeps. An enemy has done this: a plain answer to the great question of the origin of evil."
          }
        ],
        "say": "The weeds in your life and your church are not from the hand of God — they were sown in a sleeping hour, and the call is to wake.",
        "do": "Point to one unguarded place — a habit, a relationship, a corner of the church — where vigilance has lapsed, and watch there again."
      },
      {
        "landing": "The harvest sorts it",
        "claim": "The mixed field is not the last word — there is a harvest, a real and final sorting that belongs to God, where the weeds are bound and the righteous shine like the sun.",
        "witnesses": [
          {
            "author": "John Chrysostom",
            "tradition": "greek-patristic",
            "work": "Catena Aurea",
            "mode": "text",
            "reading": "Behold the love of God: ready to show mercy, slow to punish. When he sows, he sows himself; when he punishes, he punishes by others — by the angels."
          },
          {
            "author": "Remigius of Auxerre",
            "tradition": "medieval",
            "work": "Catena Aurea",
            "mode": "text",
            "reading": "The harvest is the day of judgment, when the good are parted from the evil by the ministry of angels; the righteous, who now shine for an example, shall then shine as the sun."
          }
        ],
        "say": "Leave the verdict to the harvest — it is coming, it is God's, and the wheat will shine in it.",
        "do": "Turn anxious or vengeful hearts from rendering verdicts now to entrusting the harvest to God — and to living as wheat."
      }
    ],
    "synthesis": {
      "convergence": "The readings show a sovereignty that looks like forbearance — a God who judges with mildness and teaches his people to be kind (Wisdom 12), who abounds in steadfast love (Psalm 86), who waits with a groaning creation that hopes for what it cannot yet see (Romans 8) — over a field where wheat and weeds grow tangled until harvest.",
      "claim": "The God who could uproot every weed chooses patience and kindness instead — and asks the same costly patience of you.",
      "here": "For people quick to sort the world (and the church) into the righteous and the villains, the move is the hard patience of hope: leave the harvest to God, extend to the 'weeds' the mildness God extends to you, and wait — with the whole groaning creation — for a verdict that is not yours to render."
    },
    "subtract": "Three doors, three landings — patience (let both grow), vigilance (while they slept), hope-and-warning (the harvest sorts it). Choose one for your people and let the others go.",
    "source": "Catena Aurea (Aquinas; J.H. Newman tr., public domain) + Wesley's Explanatory Notes, retrieved from ~/reception-corpus — attested, not authored.",
